---
// website/src/pages/index.astro — the site's front page (website plan, Task W1.4; redesign
// part P8, findings RF20 and RF21; changes spec of 2026-10-18, items 3 and 4).
//
// **The order is the page's argument**, and the review of this branch asked for it in so
// many words: the promise and the one thing to do about it, then the example of it through
// the reader's own agent — the conversation, and the one command — then what a run is, the
// four steps drawn, then the commands.
//
// **The chat is the first block under the promise and the diagram is the second** (review
// round 5, which found the page's own picture of a run standing between a reader and the
// thing they are being offered). What the page is selling is a conversation with the agent
// the reader already has, so the illustration of that conversation is what they meet first;
// the flow is what the agent does inside one of its turns, and it reads as the explanation
// under the example. The earlier arrangement put the diagram first for item 4's acceptance —
// that the diagram is the most visually prominent element in its section, which a picture
// four blocks down is not — and that still holds: the flow is its own section directly under
// the chat, drawn at the full width of the page's column, and nothing after it competes with
// it. Item 4 orders the diagram against the *promise*, not against the chat.
//
// Every other thing the front page could say is a section of its own with a section's own
// facts (W2.x, W3.x), and a placeholder here would be a sentence the product cannot back
// (F50). What the page reads, it reads through the content pipeline (W1.3) — it holds no fact
// of its own.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro'
import ArchitectureDiagram from '../components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro'
import AgentChat from '../components/AgentChat.astro'
import Benefits from '../components/Benefits.astro'
import HomeInstall from '../components/HomeInstall.astro'
---

<Base title="Fugaro">
  <Hero />
  {/* Divs rather than sections: the flow is a figure and the chat is a figure, and a
      `<section>` around one of them with no heading of its own would be a landmark a reader
      has to skip through with no name on it. */}
  <div class="home-chat">
    <AgentChat />
  </div>
  <div class="home-flow">
    <ArchitectureDiagram />
  </div>
  {/* The benefits, between what a run is and how to get one: the flow says what happens to a
      task, and the README's four reasons are what a reader weighs before they install it
      (the owner's feedback of 2026-10-19). `Benefits.astro` renders the section and its
      heading itself, so it is its own landmark rather than a figure in a div. */}
  <Benefits />
  <HomeInstall />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The two figures sit on the same column as the hero and the install paths (W1.2's tokens;
     the spacing is the layout's, not a number of this page's own). `main:has(> .hero)` drops
     `main`'s padding on this page, so each top-level block carries the gutter **and the room
     below itself** — which is what makes the space between two blocks the block above's own
     bottom padding, and why no block adds a top padding of its own on top of it (review round
     5: the install section's `--space-lg` over the flow's `--space-3xl` was 144 px of blank
     page between the last card and the "Install" heading, and `base-styles.mjs`'s
     `homeRhythmFailures` now refuses a pair of blocks that both pad one gap). */
  .home-chat,
  .home-flow,
  .home-benefits {
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
  }
</style>
